1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,070
So now we're going to apply the thinking hat method to our grocery store example.

2
00:00:05,070 --> 00:00:09,165
We'll be discussing three ideas from earlier on in the sprint.

3
00:00:09,165 --> 00:00:13,365
The first idea is navigate and providing turn-by-turn

4
00:00:13,365 --> 00:00:18,165
directions to shoppers inside a store based on what is on their shopping list,

5
00:00:18,165 --> 00:00:21,795
the second idea is recommended products so recommending

6
00:00:21,795 --> 00:00:25,380
additional items based on what's in a user's cart,

7
00:00:25,380 --> 00:00:28,170
and the last concept is autopilot,

8
00:00:28,170 --> 00:00:33,615
so allowing users to subscribe to automatically purchase specific goods.

9
00:00:33,615 --> 00:00:35,580
So to get started,

10
00:00:35,580 --> 00:00:37,275
what I'm going to do is I'm going to put on

11
00:00:37,275 --> 00:00:41,510
the blue hat and we're going to talk a little bit about process.

12
00:00:41,510 --> 00:00:44,390
The goal here is to really make sure that we're all

13
00:00:44,390 --> 00:00:47,475
aligned in terms of structuring the conversation.

14
00:00:47,475 --> 00:00:52,865
So the outcome that we're trying to get to is we really want to understand and align

15
00:00:52,865 --> 00:00:59,000
on which is the right idea for us to continue bringing through the rest of the sprint,

16
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:02,630
through prototyping and through user validation.

17
00:01:02,630 --> 00:01:05,270
The other goal here is to just make sure that we're

18
00:01:05,270 --> 00:01:08,210
wearing the right hats in the right order.

19
00:01:08,210 --> 00:01:13,730
So the sequencing that we'll be using is listed here since we know that

20
00:01:13,730 --> 00:01:16,400
this is the right order to wear the hats when

21
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:19,525
you're comparing different ideas against each other.

22
00:01:19,525 --> 00:01:23,160
So now I'm going to take off this lovely blue hat,

23
00:01:23,160 --> 00:01:25,925
and I'm going to put on this white hat.

24
00:01:25,925 --> 00:01:29,480
We're going to talk a little bit about data.

25
00:01:29,480 --> 00:01:33,440
So things here to focus on are what are the things that we know,

26
00:01:33,440 --> 00:01:35,705
what are the types of things that we want to find out,

27
00:01:35,705 --> 00:01:38,450
and we're not really at the point where we're taking sides yet.

28
00:01:38,450 --> 00:01:43,240
So we know that consumers visit grocery stores about 1.6 times per week,

29
00:01:43,240 --> 00:01:45,830
and we also know that the average consumer spends

30
00:01:45,830 --> 00:01:49,010
about 30 minutes in our store during each visit.

31
00:01:49,010 --> 00:01:54,385
This doesn't include travel time and they also usually purchase around 13 items.

32
00:01:54,385 --> 00:01:59,060
We also know that on average our stores carry 50,000 items.

33
00:01:59,060 --> 00:02:00,965
Now that we talked about what we know,

34
00:02:00,965 --> 00:02:03,860
we need to spend a little bit of time talking about things that

35
00:02:03,860 --> 00:02:07,085
would be helpful for us to arrive at a conclusion.

36
00:02:07,085 --> 00:02:11,090
So some things that we need to find out are things like,

37
00:02:11,090 --> 00:02:14,180
do consumers prefer shopping in a store?

38
00:02:14,180 --> 00:02:16,160
Do they prefer shopping online?

39
00:02:16,160 --> 00:02:21,260
Are consumers usually tending to purchase the same types of products and brands?

40
00:02:21,260 --> 00:02:23,450
Then for both of those questions,

41
00:02:23,450 --> 00:02:27,560
do these answers vary based on the types of goods that are being purchased?

42
00:02:27,560 --> 00:02:33,390
For example, cleaning supplies versus something like fresh produce.

